<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>Birge, B. (2002) Women, Property, and Confucian Reaction in Sung and Yüan China (960–1368). Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.</li><li>Brook, T. (2010) The Troubled Empire: China in the Yuan and Ming Dynasties. Cambridge, MA: Belknap Press of Harvard University Press.</li><li>Franke, H. and Twitchett, D. (eds) (1994) The Cambridge History of China, Volume 6: Alien Regimes and Border States, 907–1368. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.</li><li>Langlois, J. D. (ed.) (1981) China under Mongol Rule. Princeton: Princeton University Press.</li><li>Mote, F. W. (1999) Imperial China, 900–1800. Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press.</li><li>Rossabi, M. (1988) Khubilai Khan: His Life and Times. Berkeley: University of California Press.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: the registered population in the censuses of about 1290–1300; historians think many more people, chiefly in the south, went unregistered.</p></section>
